as is custom the february education  youth culture and sports council will  focus on education issues and on how  these can contribute to economic growth  jobs and social cohesion  in support of the so-called european  semester which is the six-month cycle of  economic policy coordination in the eu  launched by the commission's annual  growth survey  ministers will focus on innovative  education and training systems across  the eu to improve skills performance and  ensure that young people and low skilled  adults acquire the kind of skills and  competencies relevant to today's labor  market  ministers will also hold a public debate  on skills and employability in the light  of the results of two recent surveys by  the oecd  one which measured the performance of 15  year olds in reading maths and science  and an international assessment on the  skills of the adult population  the results of their discussion will  feed into the wider debate that would  take place during the next spring  european council in march
 

